::ALG2_U02_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the degree of f(x) = 5x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 8? {
=2 #Correct. The highest exponent is 2.
~5 #This is the leading coefficient, not the degree.
~3 #This is part of the linear coefficient.
~8 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic has vertex (-2, 7) and opens downward. What is its maximum value? {
~-2 #This is the x-coordinate where the maximum occurs.
=7 #Correct. The y-coordinate of the vertex is the maximum when the graph opens downward.
~2 #This changes the sign of the vertex x-coordinate.
~-7 #This changes the sign of the output value.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which describes f(x) = -3x<sup>5</sup> + 2x? {
~falls left, rises right #That would require a positive leading coefficient.
~both ends rise #Both ends rising requires even degree with positive leading coefficient.
=rises left, falls right #Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
~both ends fall #Both ends falling requires even degree with negative leading coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>For f(x) = (x - 2)<sup>2</sup>(x + 5), which zero has even multiplicity?<br><br>Question:<br>x = 2 {
~This zero has multiplicity 1. #x = 5
~This reverses the sign of x + 5. #x = -2
~This reverses the sign of x - 2. #
=The repeated factor (x - 2)<sup>2</sup> gives zero x = 2 with even multiplicity. #Correct. x = -5
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Which feature helps sketch f(x) = (x + 1)(x - 4)?<br><br>Question:<br>zeros at -1 and 4 {
=Each factor gives a zero by setting it equal to 0. #Correct. zeros at 1 and -4
~These signs are reversed. #vertex at (-1, 4)
~The factors do not directly give this vertex. #no x-intercepts
~The factored form shows two x-intercepts. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>A model P(t) has P(0) = 120. What does 120 represent?<br><br>Question:<br>the initial output {
~A zero is an input where the output is 0. #the degree
=P(0) is the y-intercept or starting output. #Correct. a zero
~The output at 0 does not identify degree. #the end behavior
~End behavior describes very large inputs. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 1 >= 0. {
~-1 <= x <= 1 #That interval gives values on or below the x-axis.
~-1 < x < 1 #This excludes endpoints and chooses the wrong interval.
=x <= -1 or x >= 1 #Correct. The graph is on or above the x-axis outside the zeros and includes them.
~all real numbers #Values between -1 and 1 make the expression negative.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.5<br><br>Question:<br>For y < x<sup>2</sup> + 1, the boundary is {
~solid #Solid would include equality.
~vertical #The boundary is a parabola.
~not graphed #A boundary graph is needed.
=dashed #Correct. Strict inequality excludes equality, so the boundary is dashed.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y <= -x<sup>2</sup> + 4, shade {
=below the parabola #Correct. The symbol <= means y-values less than or equal to the boundary.
~above the parabola #Above would match a greater-than inequality.
~only the vertex #The solution is a region, not one point.
~outside the zeros #That describes an interval, not a two-variable region.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is a quadratic polynomial? {
~4x<sup>3</sup> - 1 #This is cubic.
=3x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 1 #Correct. A quadratic polynomial has degree 2.
~7x - 2 #This is linear.
~5 #This is constant.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = (x - 3)<sup>2</sup> - 5, what is the vertex? {
~(-3, -5) #The sign inside the parentheses is reversed.
~(3, 5) #The k-value is -5.
=(3, -5) #Correct. Vertex form (x - h)<sup>2</sup> + k gives vertex (h, k).
~(-5, 3) #This switches the coordinates.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What is the end behavior of f(x) = 4x<sup>6</sup> - 2x? {
~both ends fall #That would require a negative leading coefficient.
~left falls, right rises #That is positive odd-degree behavior.
~left rises, right falls #That is negative odd-degree behavior.
=both ends rise #Correct. Even degree with positive leading coefficient rises on both ends.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>For f(x) = (x + 3)<sup>3</sup>(x - 1), which zero has multiplicity 3?<br><br>Question:<br>x = -3 {
=The factor x + 3 has exponent 3. #Correct. x = 3
~This reverses the sign. #x = 1
~This zero has multiplicity 1. #x = -1
~This reverses the sign of x - 1. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>What does a zero with odd multiplicity usually do on a graph?<br><br>Question:<br>crosses the x-axis {
~That is typical of even multiplicity. #creates a hole
=Odd multiplicity usually changes sign at the zero. #Correct. touches and turns
~Polynomial graphs are continuous. #creates a vertical asymptote
~Polynomial graphs do not have vertical asymptotes. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>A height model has zeros at t = 0 and t = 6. What does t = 6 likely represent?<br><br>Question:<br>landing time {
~Initial height is H(0). #maximum height
~Maximum height occurs at the vertex. #the degree
=A zero height after launch often represents when the object returns to the ground. #Correct. initial height
~A zero does not identify degree. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 9 < 0. {
~x < -3 or x > 3 #That is where the graph is above the x-axis.
~-3 <= x <= 3 #This includes endpoints where the value is 0.
~all real numbers #Outside the interval, the expression is positive.
=-3 < x < 3 #Correct. The upward-opening graph is below the x-axis between the zeros, excluding endpoints.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.5<br><br>Question:<br>For y >= x<sup>2</sup> - 2, the boundary should be {
=solid #Correct. The symbol >= includes equality, so boundary points are included.
~dashed #Dashed is used for strict inequalities.
~horizontal #The boundary is a parabola.
~removed #The boundary is part of the graph.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y > x<sup>2</sup> - 5, shade {
~below the parabola #Below matches y less than.
=above the parabola #Correct. y greater than means points with y-values above the boundary.
~on the parabola only #Strict inequality excludes the boundary.
~inside the zeros only #This is a two-variable region.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the leading coefficient of f(x) = -6x<sup>2</sup> + x - 4? {
~2 #This is the degree.
~1 #This is the coefficient of the linear term.
=-6 #Correct. The leading coefficient belongs to the highest-degree term.
~-4 #This is the constant term.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the axis of symmetry for f(x) = (x + 4)<sup>2</sup> + 9? {
~x = 4 #The sign inside the parentheses is reversed.
~y = 9 #This is a horizontal line.
~x = 9 #The y-coordinate is not the axis value.
=x = -4 #Correct. The axis of symmetry passes through the vertex x-coordinate.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which polynomial has both ends falling? {
=-2x<sup>4</sup> + 3 #Correct. Even degree with a negative leading coefficient falls on both ends.
~2x<sup>4</sup> - 3 #This rises on both ends.
~2x<sup>3</sup> - 3 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
~-2x<sup>3</sup> + 3 #Odd degree has opposite end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Which factor gives the zero x = 8?<br><br>Question:<br>x - 8 {
~This gives x = -8. #8x
=Setting x - 8 = 0 gives x = 8. #Correct. x + 8
~This gives x = 0. #x - 1
~This gives x = 1. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>For f(x) = (x - 6)<sup>2</sup>, what is the x-intercept?<br><br>Question:<br>(6, 0) {
~This reverses the sign. #(0, 6)
~This is not an x-intercept. #(2, 0)
=The zero is x = 6, so the x-intercept is (6, 0). #Correct. (-6, 0)
~The exponent gives multiplicity. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>If a model gives a negative time solution, what should usually happen?<br><br>Question:<br>reject it in the context {
~Algebraic solutions must be checked against context. #make it positive
~Changing the sign changes the solution. #use it as the maximum
~A negative input is not automatically a maximum. #
=Negative time is usually not meaningful for real elapsed time. #Correct. keep it automatically
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 5x + 6 = 0. {
=x = -2 and x = -3 #Correct. Factoring gives (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0.
~x = 2 and x = 3 #These signs are reversed.
~x = 5 and x = 6 #These are coefficient and constant values.
~x = 0 and x = 6 #These do not satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.5<br><br>Question:<br>For y <= -x<sup>2</sup> + 3, which boundary is correct? {
~dashed parabola #Dashed would mean equality is not included.
=solid parabola #Correct. The symbol <= includes equality, and the boundary is a parabola.
~solid line #The boundary is not linear.
~no boundary #A boundary is required.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y < -x<sup>2</sup> + 1, shade {
~above the parabola #Above would match y greater than.
~only on the parabola #Strict inequality excludes boundary points.
=below the parabola #Correct. y less than means points below the boundary.
~left of the y-axis #The inequality describes vertical shading relative to the curve.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which value is f(0) for f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 6? {
~0 #The input is 0, but the output is 6.
~-5 #This is the linear coefficient.
~1 #This is the coefficient of x<sup>2</sup>.
=6 #Correct. Substituting x = 0 leaves the constant term 6.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic opens upward and has vertex (1, -4). What is its minimum value? {
=-4 #Correct. For an upward-opening parabola, the vertex y-coordinate is the minimum.
~1 #This is the x-coordinate where the minimum occurs.
~4 #This changes the sign.
~-1 #This uses the wrong coordinate.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which leading term gives left end down and right end up? {
~-5x<sup>7</sup> #This rises left and falls right.
=5x<sup>7</sup> #Correct. Positive odd-degree leading terms fall left and rise right.
~5x<sup>8</sup> #This rises on both ends.
~-5x<sup>8</sup> #This falls on both ends.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>If a graph touches and turns at x = -2, what multiplicity is most likely?<br><br>Question:<br>even multiplicity {
~Odd multiplicity usually crosses. #no multiplicity
~A real zero has a multiplicity. #negative multiplicity
=Touching and turning is typical of even multiplicity. #Correct. odd multiplicity
~Multiplicity is a positive integer. #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Which zeros come from f(x) = x(x - 5)(x + 4)?<br><br>Question:<br>x = 0, 5, and -4 {
~The signs of the last two zeros are reversed. #x = 5 and -4 only
~The factor x gives zero 0 too. #x = 1, 5, and 4
~The coefficient of x is not a zero. #
=Set each factor equal to zero. #Correct. x = 0, -5, and 4
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>A model has maximum output 80 at x = 3. What does 80 represent?<br><br>Question:<br>the greatest modeled output {
=The y-value at the maximum point is the maximum output. #Correct. the input where maximum occurs
~That is x = 3. #a zero of the model
~A zero has output 0. #the starting value
~Starting value is f(0). #
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves (x - 2)(x + 4) >= 0? {
~-4 <= x <= 2 #That interval gives nonpositive values.
=x <= -4 or x >= 2 #Correct. The product is nonnegative outside the zeros and includes endpoints.
~-4 < x < 2 #This is the wrong interval and excludes endpoints.
~all real numbers #Between the zeros the product is negative.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.5<br><br>Question:<br>For y > x<sup>2</sup> + 2, is the boundary included? {
~Yes, because it is a parabola. #Graph type does not decide boundary inclusion.
~Yes, because y is positive. #The sign of y does not decide boundary inclusion.
=No, because the inequality is strict. #Correct. The symbol > does not include equality.
~No, because all parabolas are dashed. #Only strict inequalities use dashed boundaries.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y >= x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
~(0, -2) #-2 is below -1.
~(2, 0) #At x = 2, the boundary is 3.
~(1, -2) #At x = 1, the boundary is 0.
=(0, 0) #Correct. At x = 0, the boundary value is -1 and 0 >= -1 is true.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which function is written in factored form? {
=f(x) = (x - 2)(x + 5) #Correct. Factored form shows a product of factors.
~f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> + 3x - 10 #This is standard form.
~f(x) = (x - 1)<sup>2</sup> + 4 #This is vertex form.
~f(x) = 2x + 5 #This is linear form.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.4<br><br>Question:<br>For f(x) = x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3, what are the x-intercepts? {
~(-1, 0) and (-3, 0) #These signs are reversed.
=(1, 0) and (3, 0) #Correct. Factoring gives (x - 1)(x - 3).
~(0, 1) and (0, 3) #These are not x-intercepts.
~(4, 0) and (3, 0) #The coefficient 4 is not a zero.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.8<br><br>Question:<br>What controls the end behavior of f(x) = -2x<sup>6</sup> + 9x<sup>2</sup> - 1? {
~9x<sup>2</sup> #This is a lower-degree term.
~-1 #The constant does not control end behavior.
=-2x<sup>6</sup> #Correct. The highest-degree term dominates.
~all terms equally #The leading term controls end behavior.
}

::ALG2_U02_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Unit 2 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A graph rises on both ends and touches the x-axis at x = 4. Which factor could explain the touch point? {
~(x + 4)<sup>2</sup> #This gives zero -4.
~(x - 4) #This gives zero 4 but usually crosses.
~(x + 4) #This gives zero -4 and usually crosses.
=(x - 4)<sup>2</sup> #Correct. The factor gives zero 4 and even multiplicity, so it touches.
}

